# When would parents feel proud of their children?
I think parents feel proud of their children whenever they see them grow into responsible and independent individuals. Of course, academic achievements, good grades, or career success can make parents feel proud, but I don't think those are the only things that matter.

In many cases, parents are even more proud when their children show good values, such as kindness, honesty, and respect for others. For example, helping family members, treating people politely, or overcoming difficulties independently can make parents feel that their efforts in raising their children have paid off.

So overall, I think parents value both achievements and good character, and sometimes small everyday actions can mean even more than major accomplishments.

# Should parents reward children? Why and How?
Yes, I believe parents should reward their children, but it needs to be done in a sensible way. Rewards can be a great source of motivation and encourage children to develop positive habits, such as studying hard, helping with household chores, or behaving responsibly.

However, I don't think rewards should always be material things like money or expensive gifts. Sometimes, simple praise, words of encouragement, or spending quality time together can be even more effective. These kinds of rewards help children feel appreciated without making them overly dependent on material incentives.

I also think parents should focus on rewarding effort rather than just results. For example, if a child works hard for an exam but doesn't get the highest score, their effort should still be recognized. This can help children develop perseverance and a growth mindset.

Overall, when used appropriately, rewards can boost children's confidence and encourage positive behavior.
